[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-cloud-itonami-hygiene-access-gtm", :doc/title "cloud-itonami-hygiene-access — go-to-market: affordable NaOCl / IPMP hygiene for water-scarce and poor-sanitation markets", :doc/path "90-docs/business/cloud-itonami-hygiene-access-gtm.edn", :doc/body "# cloud-itonami-hygiene-access — go-to-market: affordable NaOCl / IPMP hygiene for water-scarce and poor-sanitation markets\n\n**As-of**: 2026-07-18\n**Author**: Jun Kawasaki (drafted with Claude)\n**Status**: draft — a business design layered on a real, tested actor implementation; not a claim of live sales, regulatory clearance, or manufacturing\n**Builds on**: `90-docs/adr/2607182900-cloud-itonami-hygiene-access-actor.edn` (registration ADR, actor architecture),\n`90-docs/adr/2607113000-swachh-actor-india-waste-sanitation-actor.edn` (the investigation that first surfaced this gap),\n`orgs/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-hygiene-access/products.edn` (the 4-SKU catalog and its ground-truth market-approval data)\n\n## 0. What this document is\n\nA go-to-market narrative for the `cloud-itonami-hygiene-access` actor's commercial catalog: why these\ntwo actives, why these three regions, how the four SKUs are priced and packaged, which markets to enter\nfirst, which distribution channels fit which product, and the specific ethical guardrail (marketing-claim\nsubstantiation) that differentiates this from the extractive \"sell anything to poor markets\" pattern this\ndesign deliberately avoids. It is not a restatement of the registration ADR — the ADR documents the actor's\narchitecture and governor checks; this document argues the *business* case for what the actor's own\ncatalog already encodes.\n\n## 1. Positioning: why sodium hypochlorite and IPMP, why India / Gulf-MENA / South & Southeast Asia\n\n### 1.1 The burden this targets\n\nUnsafe drinking water, inadequate sanitation, and poor hand hygiene remain among the largest preventable\ndisease burdens in the regions this catalog targets. WHO/UNICEF Joint Monitoring Programme (JMP) tracking\nof \"safely managed\" drinking water and sanitation access has consistently shown the largest shortfalls\nconcentrated in South Asia, parts of the Middle East/North Africa, and Southeast Asia — the same footprint\nas this actor's three target regions. Diarrheal disease linked to unsafe water, sanitation, and hygiene\n(WASH) remains a leading cause of preventable death in children under five in these geographies, and\nhousehold-level point-of-use water treatment plus routine handwashing are two of the best-evidenced,\nlowest-cost interventions available — not aspirational public-health theory, but decades-old field practice.\n\n### 1.2 Why these two actives specifically\n\n- **Sodium hypochlorite (次亜塩素酸ナトリウム, NaOCl)** is the active behind the WHO/CDC \"Safe Water\n  System\" household water-treatment model: a dilute stock solution, a few drops dosed per liter of\n  drinking water by the end user. It is off-patent, cheap to produce at low concentration, chemically\n  well-understood, and has decades of social-marketing precedent in exactly these markets (e.g. Population\n  Services International-style \"Safe Water\" branded chlorine solutions sold at BOP price points across\n  South Asia and Sub-Saharan Africa since the 1990s–2000s). The same active, at a different concentration,\n  is also the active ingredient behind ubiquitous low-cost surface disinfectants — a second product line\n  built on one supply chain.\n- **Isopropylmethylphenol (IPMP / o-cymen-5-ol)** is a long-established antibacterial active used in OTC\n  medicated soap formulations at low concentration (0.1–0.3%). It is cheap, widely regulatory-precedented\n  in soap/handwash formulations, and pairs naturally with a hand-hygiene behavior-change push — the\n  second pillar of the WASH intervention pair (safe water + handwashing), not a redundant third product\n  line.\n\nBoth actives are chosen specifically because they are **cheap, off-patent, and already precedented** —\nthis is not a novel-chemistry bet, it is a distribution and go-to-market bet on well-understood actives\nthat have simply never been assembled into one commercialization chain (formulation → packaging →\npricing → claims → channel) inside this fleet before (see `90-docs/adr/2607182900-...` §Context for the\ngap this closes: `etzhayyim/com-etzhayyim-yakushi` formulates NaOCl but ships no IPMP SKU and has no\nmarket/GTM layer at all; `cloud-itonami-isic-2023` is a generic soap/detergent plant coordinator with no\nactive-ingredient-level formulation or market targeting).\n\n### 1.3 Why these three regions\n\n- **India**: the single largest concentration of both population and WASH-access gaps in the catalog's\n  footprint; deep existing informal-retail and microfinance/self-help-group infrastructure that BOP\n  hygiene products have historically used as a distribution backbone (this is the same distribution logic\n  `com-etzhayyim-swachh-actor`'s adjacent waste/sanitation work operates in).\n- **Gulf/Arabia/MENA**: a deliberately different market logic from India/South Asia — higher per-capita\n  income in the Gulf states but acute, structural water scarcity (desalination-dependent supply, hot\n  climate accelerating surface-contamination risk), plus lower-income MENA/North Africa markets (Egypt)\n  where affordability constraints resemble South Asia more than the Gulf. The catalog's surface\n  disinfectant SKU leans into this region (`IN`/`SA`/`AE`/`EG` target set) because hot-climate,\n  water-scarce households have a distinct need for a no-dilution, ready-to-use surface product.\n- **South & Southeast Asia** (Bangladesh, Pakistan, Indonesia, Philippines): comparable WASH-access\n  gaps to India, similarly dense informal-retail and microfinance-adjacent distribution infrastructure,\n  and — for the water-purification-drops SKU specifically — direct precedent from real-world Safe Water\n  System deployments in this exact geography.\n\n## 2. The 4-SKU catalog: pricing and packaging rationale\n\nAll prices are USDC-minor (6dp) and were programmatically checked against the actor's own governor\ntables (`hygaccess.registry/price-ceiling-minor`, `efficacy-window-pct`, `valid-packaging-formats`,\n`substantiated-claims`) — nothing in this catalog is a formulation, price, or claim the actor's own\n`:propose-market-entry` / `:propose-marketing-claim` checks would reject.\n\n| SKU | Active | Conc. | Format | Price | Ceiling | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Water purification drops | NaOCl | 1.0% stock | `small-bottle-50ml` | $0.35 | $0.50 | 50ml dropper bottle doses roughly 1,000L of household water at label dosing — a single unit lasts a household weeks to months, which is the affordability lever (low per-unit price, long per-unit duration) that makes point-of-use water treatment viable at BOP income levels |\n| Surface disinfectant | NaOCl | 0.1% RTU | `small-bottle-100ml` | $0.70 | $1.00 | ready-to-use (no end-user dilution) trades a higher per-unit price against zero dosing-error risk — appropriate for the higher-income Gulf-leaning segment of its target set (`SA`/`AE`) while remaining under ceiling for `IN`/`EG` |\n| Antibacterial soap bar | IPMP | 0.15% | `sachet` | $0.15 | $0.30 | sachet/individually-wrapped format is the single most BOP-proven packaging format in this category — it matches the per-use, cash-constrained purchasing pattern (buy-what-you-need-today) that dominates informal retail in South Asia and is the lowest absolute price point in the catalog, intentionally, since soap is the highest-frequency-purchase item of the four |\n| Antibacterial liquid soap | IPMP | 0.2% | `small-bottle-50ml` | $0.25 | $0.30 | kept deliberately small (50ml, not a larger \"value\" bottle) specifically to stay comfortably under the product type's own affordability ceiling rather than chasing a lower per-ml unit price at a higher absolute price point that would price out the target segment |\n\nThe packaging logic across all four SKUs is the same discipline: **smaller units at lower absolute\nprice points**, even where a larger unit would have a better per-ml/per-dose unit economics, because BOP\npurchasing power is constrained by absolute cash-on-hand per purchase occasion, not by unit price\noptimization. This is why `sachet` and `small-bottle-50ml` dominate the catalog rather than\nfamily-size formats.\n\n## 3. Market sequencing: approved vs. pending, and why that split exists\n\nThe actor's own seeded ground-truth table (`hygaccess.store/sample-data!`, read directly from the\n`products.edn` `:hygaccess.product/target-countries-approved` / `-pending` fields, not assumed) currently\ncarries:\n\n- **Approved**: India (`IN`), Bangladesh (`BD`), Indonesia (`ID`), Philippines (`PH`), Saudi Arabia (`SA`),\n  United Arab Emirates (`AE`)\n- **Pending local regulatory review**: Pakistan (`PK`), Egypt (`EG`)\n\nThis is a **go-to-market roadmap superimposed on a market-approval ground truth**, not a claim that every\nlisted target country is already clear to sell into. Per-SKU:\n\n- Water-purification drops target `IN`/`BD`/`PK`/`ID`/`PH` — four of five approved, `PK` pending.\n- Surface disinfectant targets `IN`/`SA`/`AE`/`EG` — three of four approved, `EG` pending.\n- Both soap SKUs target the widest set (`IN`/`BD`/`PK`/`ID`/`PH`[+`EG` for the bar]) — four of five (or six)\n  approved, `PK`(+`EG`) pending.\n\n**Sequencing implication**: the six approved markets are where the actor's `:propose-market-entry` op can\nactually clear the `market-not-approved` governor check today; `PK` and `EG` are correctly held by that\nsame check until local regulatory review completes. This is not a bug to route around — it is the intended\nbehavior of a ground-truth, not-self-report gate in a safety-critical, regulated domain. Any real-world\nsequencing plan should treat `PK`/`EG` entry as gated on securing the applicable local regulatory clearance\nfirst (see §6), not as a market-entry proposal to push through.\n\n## 4. Channel strategy: when each channel applies\n\nFive distribution channels appear across the four SKUs, deliberately not interchangeably:\n\n- **`ngo-who-program`** — water-purification drops only. This SKU's WHO/CDC Safe Water System framing is\n  most credible distributed through the same NGO/public-health-program channel that has historically\n  carried Safe Water System-style products, rather than open retail, where dosing guidance and health\n  messaging travel with the product.\n- **`government-procurement`** — water-purification drops, paired with the NGO channel. Public-health-scale\n  water treatment (municipal emergency response, refugee/displacement settings, rural public-health\n  programs) is a genuine bulk-procurement use case distinct from retail household purchase.\n  the two soap SKUs (`microfinance-social-enterprise` + `licensed-informal-retail-aggregator`) — market\n  reach through social enterprises operating on microfinance rails, plus small kiosk/informal retail —\n  matches how soap actually moves at BOP price points in these markets today; a direct-to-consumer or\n  organized-retail channel would miss the density of actual purchase points.\n- **`licensed-informal-retail-aggregator`** — surface disinfectant and both soap SKUs. This channel exists\n  specifically to formalize (via the `distribution-channel-partner not licensed` governor gate — check #18)\n  what would otherwise be an unregulated informal-retail supply chain: the partner must be independently\n  verified as `:licensed?` and actually serving the declared channel before any market-entry proposal can\n  clear.\n- **`institutional-bulk`** — surface disinfectant only, alongside informal retail. Institutional buyers\n  (clinics, schools, small hospitality) are a distinct high-volume, lower-margin-per-unit buyer class from\n  household retail.\n- **`microfinance-social-enterprise`** — both soap SKUs. Reuses the same self-help-group/microfinance\n  distribution rails that BOP hygiene and sanitation products (including the adjacent `swachh-actor`\n  domain) already lean on in these markets.\n\nNo SKU uses every channel — the catalog deliberately matches channel to product type and use case rather\nthan blanket-distributing everything everywhere.\n\n## 5. Marketing-claim substantiation: an explicit trust and ethics differentiator, not boilerplate\n\nEvery `:propose-marketing-claim` proposal is checked against a **closed, pre-approved,\nWHO/CDC-style substantiated-claims set** (`hygaccess.registry/substantiated-claims`) — never taken on the\nadvisor's self-report that a claim is \"backed by data.\" This is governor check #19, and it is a HARD,\nalways-escalating check (marketing-claim proposals always route to human sign-off regardless of confidence).\n\nThis matters specifically because the target population is a **vulnerable population by definition of the\nmarket**: low-income, often low-literacy, in some cases displaced or facing acute WASH crisis, purchasing a\nhealth-adjacent product on trust in packaging and word-of-mouth rather than on independent verification.\nThis is exactly the population that predatory or careless marketing of hygiene/health products has\nhistorically targeted with unsubstantiated \"cures disease X\" claims. The closed claim set is a **structural**\nguardrail against that pattern, not a compliance afterthought: the four SKUs' actual claims are all\nnarrowly scoped and behaviorally honest —\n\n- \"reduces waterborne bacterial and viral pathogens when used per labeled dosing\" (not \"purifies water\" or\n  \"prevents cholera\")\n- \"disinfects hard non-porous surfaces\" (not \"kills all germs\" or \"protects your family from disease\")\n- \"reduces transient bacteria on hands with proper handwashing technique\" (explicitly conditions the claim\n  on correct technique, not a passive guarantee)\n- \"supports hand-hygiene behavior alongside adequate water access\" (explicitly frames the product as one\n  component of a hygiene practice, not a substitute for water access)\n\nThis claim discipline is a genuine differentiator versus how low-cost hygiene products are frequently\nmarketed in these exact markets in the real world, and is treated in the actor's own architecture as a\nfirst-class check, not an afterthought (see registration ADR §Decision, check #19, and the repo's own\nREADME \"Marketing-claim substantiation — a first-class ethical guardrail\" section).\n\n## 6. What this is / isn't\n\n**This is**: a governed coordination-and-proposal layer (advisor + independent governor + append-only\nledger) plus a business/go-to-market design — a real, tested actor (87 tests / 283 assertions, 0\nfailures/errors; 0 lint errors/warnings) with a real, internally-consistent commercial catalog (4 SKUs,\nall fields checked against the actor's own closed registry tables) and a real go-to-market narrative\n(this document).\n\n**This is NOT**:\n- **Not a real regulatory approval.** The actor's `market-entry-approvals` ground-truth table records\n  approval *status* per country; it does not itself grant regulatory market access. Any real deployment\n  into any of the six \"approved\" markets in this catalog would still require the applicable actual local\n  regulatory clearance before market entry — e.g. India CDSCO/BIS (Central Drugs Standard Control\n  Organisation / Bureau of Indian Standards) for a chemical/hygiene product, the applicable GCC conformity\n  body for Gulf states, or the relevant ASEAN member-state regulator for Indonesia/Philippines. This\n  document does not claim any of those clearances have been obtained, and the `PK`/`EG` \"pending\" markets\n  are explicitly held by the actor's own governor until such review completes.\n- **Not real manufacturing.** No formulation batch in this system corresponds to an actual production\n  run; `:log-production-batch` is administrative data logging, not a chemical-manufacturing control system,\n  and the actor explicitly does not control or actuate any formulation/filling-line equipment.\n- **Not literal live sales.** A `:propose-market-entry` commit is a DRAFT go-to-market record — target\n  country, price point, distribution channel, and channel partner bundled into one proposal — not an\n  executed sale, payment, or logistics dispatch. There is no payment or hardware-dispatch pathway in this\n  actor's scaffold.\n- **Not a claim that distribution-channel partners are actually under contract.** The `licensed?`\n  channel-partner data the governor checks against is the actor's own seeded/sample ground truth, not a\n  real signed distribution agreement.\n\nReal deployment of this program, in any of its target markets, would require: the applicable local\nregulatory clearance (CDSCO/BIS, GCC conformity bodies, ASEAN member-state regulators, as above), actual\nsigned distribution-channel-partner agreements, actual contract manufacturing capacity, and a human\nprogram operator running the actor's Phase 0→3 rollout under real supervision — none of which this\ndocument or the underlying actor build claims to have already secured.\n\n## References\n\n- `90-docs/adr/2607182900-cloud-itonami-hygiene-access-actor.edn` — registration ADR (architecture,\n  governor checks, verification)\n- `90-docs/adr/2607113000-swachh-actor-india-waste-sanitation-actor.edn` — the prior investigation that\n  first surfaced the gap this catalog closes\n- `orgs/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-hygiene-access/products.edn` — the 4-SKU catalog and its\n  target-countries-approved/-pending ground truth (source for all pricing/market data in this document)\n- `orgs/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-hygiene-access/README.md` — actor scope, \"what this actor does NOT do\"\n- `orgs/etzhayyim/com-etzhayyim-yakushi` — the existing sodium-hypochlorite formulation precedent this\n  catalog's efficacy windows partially reuse\n", :doc/source-format "edn", :doc/doc_type "doc"}]